Verify passes. Now I'm comparing speed against the previous version of the solver.
I rewrote `src/fom_solver.c` and `make verify` now prints `VERIFY OK`. The worst error across the 8 samples is 3.8e-12, against a tolerance of 1e-6. All of the operator checks report zero error.

**Changes:**
- **Parameter terms computed once.** The four products involving `p0` and `p1` are now computed in `fom_setup` and `fom_update_params` and stored in `work->acc`. Inside the loop, only their scaling by `tau(it)` and `eta(it)` happens each iteration.
- **Generated routines for each step.** The loop calls `fom_step_t0` (relu), `fom_step_t1` (clip) and `fom_step_su__rest` instead of zeroing buffers and accumulating with separate `fom_apply_*` calls.
- **Copied rows written directly.** Rows 128..143 of the state update are plain copies of `state[0..15]`, so the solver writes them itself using the index lists in `fom_data.h`.
- **No copy-back each iteration.** The solver swaps the `state` and `new_state` pointers instead of copying 160 values per iteration. It copies once at the end only if the result ended up in `new_state`.

It still has no dynamic memory, no `/` operator and no hardcoded numbers. Each solve starts from `FOM_STATE_INIT`.

**Speed (`make bench`, 5000 solves):** mean time is about 138 µs per solve, down from about 191 µs for the previous version at HEAD. That's roughly 28% faster.

Several claude.ai connectors (Asana, Atlassian, Box, Canva, Figma, HubSpot, Intercom, Linear, Notion, monday.com) need authorization in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. None of them were needed here.
